<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kano State</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a> State is a state in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, created in 1967, with its capital at the city of Kano. It is one of the most populous states of Nigeria and is mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> and Muslim. Its territory covers the historic kingdom and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> of Kano and neighbouring Hausa polities such as Rano, and in 2019 the state government created new emirates at Rano, Bichi, Gaya and Karaye.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kano-state.jpg" alt="Kano State" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kano_City_Aerial.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kano State is in the north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>. It was created on 27 May 1967 from the former Northern Region and borders Katsina State on the north-west, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jigawa-state">Jigawa State</a> on the north-east, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bauchi-state">Bauchi State</a> on the south-east and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaduna-state">Kaduna State</a> on the south-west. A part of it became Jigawa State in 1991. The state capital is Kano. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> make up most of the population, Hausa is the dominant language, and Hausa and Fulfulde are the official languages. The state observes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">Sharia</a> law within the framework of the Nigerian constitution. It has 44 local government areas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area was the site of several kingdoms and empires, among them the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a>, centred on Dalla Hill from about 1000 to 1349. In 1349 the Sultanate of Kano was established when Yaji I converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and became its first sultan. Kurmi Market opened in 1463 and helped the city of Kano become a centre of commercial activity in Hausaland. It is still open; its importance is reflected in the state's nickname, Centre of Commerce. The sultanate became one of the most powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> kingdoms in the 16th and 17th centuries.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulani-war">Fulani jihad</a> of 1804 to 1808 the Kano Emirate replaced the sultanate. Emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-dabo">Ibrahim Dabo</a> made administrative reforms to increase commerce, and leaders encouraged traders to move from Katsina, taking advantage of raids from the Hausa Sultanate of Maradi. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> conquered the Kano Emirate in 1903 and incorporated it into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-nigeria-protectorate">Northern Nigeria Protectorate</a>. The main ethnic groups in pre-colonial Kano were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a>, Barebari (Kanuri), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nupe-kingdom">Nupe</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a> has many large markets, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurmi">Kurmi</a>, Kantin Kwari, Sabon Gari and Dawanau, and many specialise in products such as textiles or grain. The dye industry has contributed to the economy since the 19th century. During the colonial period and for several years after independence, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundnut">groundnuts</a> grown in the state were one of the country's major sources of revenue. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">Millet</a>, cowpeas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorghum">sorghum</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> are grown for local use, and groundnuts and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> for export and industry. Industries include textiles, tanning, footwear, plastics and pharmaceuticals. Attractions include Kurmi Market, Kano's city wall, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gidan-rumfa">Gidan Rumfa</a>, the emir's palace, the Kano Museum and Dalla Hill.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kano State” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A separate Rano Emirate was created by <b>Kano State</b> in 2019, reviving the old name.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-rano">Kingdom of Rano</a></li></ul></section>
